---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";

const card = [
  ["Clase de", "1882", "The conscript class. His passport gives 22 February 1882."],
  ["(surname)", "Lerena", ""],
  ["(forenames)", "Pablo Armando", "Both of them, in his own order."],
  ["hijo de", "Juan Carlos Lerena", "His father. The answer this archive was built to find."],
  ["y de", "María Lerena", "His mother — written with the father's surname. See the caution below."],
  ["Nacido en", "Rosario de Santa Fé", "The town and the province, as on the passport."],
  ["Región Militar", "—", "Blank."],
  ["Distrito Militar N.º", "—", "Blank — because he was not enrolled in a district. He was abroad."],
  ["Oficina enroladora de", "Capetown", "The Argentine consulate at the Cape. He died at Newlands in 1950."],
  ["Matrícula N.º", "—", "Blank."],
  ["Domicilio", "—", "Blank."],
  ["Serie", "3885", "The word SERIE is what the machine index turned into a second surname."],
  ["Casilla", "—", "Blank."],
  ["Sección", "I 3322", ""],
  ["N.º de órden", "—", "Blank."],
];

const indexVsCard = [
  ["Name", "Armand Lorena", "Pablo Armando Lerena"],
  ["Birthplace", "Rosario de Lerma, Salta", "Rosario de Santa Fé"],
  ["Father", "Juan Carlos Lorena Serena", "Juan Carlos Lerena"],
  ["Mother", "María Lerena", "María Lerena"],
  ["Birth", "1882", "Clase de 1882"],
];
---
<Base title="The enrolment card"
      eyebrow="Ejército Argentino · ficha de enrolamiento · Cajón 985"
      dek="An Argentine army enrolment card, filed in a drawer marked Lera, gives the class of 1882, a birth at Rosario de Santa Fe, an enrolling office at Cape Town — and the names of Pablo Armando Lerena's father and mother.">

<section class="blk">
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0">
      <span class="chip doc">Documented</span>
      FamilySearch image group <strong>#112342450</strong>, image <strong>665 of 1,119</strong>. Volume:
      <strong>Cajón 985 (Lera)</strong>. Creator: <strong>Ejército Argentino</strong>. Collection:
      <em>Argentina, Military Records, 1911&ndash;1936</em>. Image ark
      <span class="mono">3:1:3QHK-MQL4-NQWN</span>; index ark <span class="mono">1:1:6TH8-DVXH</span>.
      Read 10 September 2026.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>It is a printed card with blank rules, filled in by hand. It is not a birth record and does not
      pretend to be one: it is the paperwork of a man of the class of 1882 registering for Argentine
      military service <em>at the consulate in Cape Town</em>. That is precisely what makes it useful.
      Everything on it was stated by someone who knew &mdash; almost certainly by Pablo Armando
      himself.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What the card says</h2>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:12rem">Printed on the card</th><th style="width:14rem">Written in by hand</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {card.map(([k, v, n]) => (
          <tr><th>{k}</th><td><strong>{v}</strong></td><td class="cap">{n}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Why it is certainly him</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Four things on this one card were already known from elsewhere, from documents that have nothing to
      do with the Argentine army:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>The class of 1882.</strong> His own passport gives 22 February 1882.</li>
      <li><strong>Nacido en Rosario de Santa Fé.</strong> The passport names that town and that province
        &mdash; and the province is the thing that separated the real man from every near miss this
        archive has logged.</li>
      <li><strong>Oficina enroladora de Capetown.</strong> He died at Newlands, Cape Town, in 1950. An
        Argentine conscript enrolling at the Cape is not a coincidence; it is a man registering with his
        consulate because he already lived there.</li>
      <li><strong>The passport's own annotation</strong> &mdash; <em>Guardia Nacional, 6º de Caballería,
        1915</em> &mdash; is the service this card is the enrolment for.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Any one of them alone would be suggestive. Together, on one card, in a drawer filed under
      <em>Lera</em>, they are conclusive.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>And from the other side of the ocean</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Pablo Armando's eldest son &mdash; named with his exact birth date in the
      <a href={u("/estate/")}>1950 liquidation account</a> &mdash; is
      <strong>Ricardo <em>Juan Carlos</em> Lerena</strong>, born 6 January 1920 at Cape Town. A grandson of
      the same names died in infancy in the Transvaal.</p>
    <p>An Argentine army card and a South African deceased estate, neither with any possible knowledge of
      the other, name the same man. <strong>The grandfather's name came out of Argentina with him and was
      given to his first son at the Cape.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>How nearly this was thrown away</h2>
  <p class="dek">The index that led here was made by a computer, and it was wrong in four places out of five.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:9rem">Field</th><th>What the index said</th><th>What the card says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {indexVsCard.map(([f, a, b]) => (
          <tr><th>{f}</th><td>{a}</td><td><strong>{b}</strong></td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p>The index dropped <em>Pablo</em>, moved him 1,400 km to Salta, turned <em>Lerena</em> into
      <em>Lorena</em>, and invented a second surname &mdash; <em>&ldquo;Serena&rdquo;</em> &mdash; out of the
      word <strong>SERIE</strong> printed on the line below the parents.</p>
    <p>This archive had already found the entry and had already written it off. The note from the previous
      round called it <strong>&ldquo;probably not him&rdquo;</strong> and listed four objections: Salta, the
      missing <em>Pablo</em>, the surname <em>Lorena</em>, and the machine indexing.
      <strong>Three of the four were artefacts of the transcription.</strong></p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <strong>The rule that follows.</strong> An index entry is a pointer, not a fact. Where a candidate has
    been excluded on indexed detail <em>alone</em>, the exclusion is worth nothing until the image is
    opened. Several exclusions in this archive rest on exactly that, and are now marked for re-checking.
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What the card does not settle</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>María Lerena is written with her husband's surname.</strong> On these cards the formula
      <em>hijo de X y de Y</em> usually gives each parent's own name &mdash; which would make her a Lerena
      by birth, and the marriage one between kin. But some clerks entered the mother under her married
      name. <strong>The card cannot decide it</strong>, and neither can this archive. Her maiden name is the
      next thing to find.</p>
    <p>And a genuine puzzle now sharpens. The Rosario baptism index has been read letter by letter, under
      any surname, for <strong>1881, 1882 and 1883</strong> &mdash; seventy-eight letter-bands &mdash; and
      it contains <strong>no Lerena at all</strong>. A boy born in Rosario in February 1882 to a father
      named Lerena is not in the cathedral's index. Either he was baptised somewhere the index does not
      reach, or under a spelling the sweep did not catch, or not baptised as an infant. That is now a
      specific question rather than a blank. <a href={u("/searched/")}>What has been searched &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What it opens</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The volume is <strong>Cajón 985 (Lera)</strong> &mdash; the cards are filed alphabetically. Every
      Argentine of enrolment age surnamed <strong>Lerena</strong> is in this same drawer, within a few
      images of this one, <em>each card naming his own parents</em>. That is a ready-made map of the family,
      and none of it has been read.</p>
    <p><a href={u("/direct-line/")}>The line, now one generation deeper &rarr;</a>
       &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href={u("/register/")}>The name register &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

</Base>
